This file describes how to install tfem on macOS.
------------------------------------------------

(See also file README.macos_arm64 for running tfem on arm64 macs)

Add the following packages/programs to macOS:

- Xcode (from Apple).
- Command line tools (from Apple).
- universal-ctags. The default ctags on macOS is not sufficient for tfem.
- fig2dev
- ghostscript
- gfortran (part of gcc)

For installing the non-Apple programs: use Homebrew

See INSTALL.linux for further instructions common to Linux.

Additional system settings:

- Use the build-in open command for viewing pdf and png files. Define the
  following environment variables in your environment files (e.g.
  $HOME/.bash_profile or $HOME/.profile):

  export PDFVIEWER='open'
  export IMAGEVIEWER='open'

  To exit the viewer use CMD-Q when viewing with viewfig or viewfigpdf.

- On my Macbook Pro I need to set:

  export SLEEP=2

  to create a delay between module building with make. I also modified the
  RANLIB macro in the file Mdefs.mk:

  RANLIB = ranlib -c

  Both modifications avoid missing symbols in libraries (at least on my Macbook
  Pro).

- I installed MacVim as editor and set VIEW as:

  export VIEW=mview

  To use tfvimdiff with MacVim add the option -m:

  tfvimdiff -m file(s)

  MacVim is available from https://github.com/macvim-dev/macvim/releases
  or via Macports.

- Running gmsh from within a shell (command line), needed for remeshing within
  a running program, requires the command gmsh to be available. One
  possibilility is to add the directory containing the executable to your PATH
  environment variable (in your .profile/.bash_profile):

  # gmsh location
  gm=/Applications/Gmsh.app/Contents
  # extend path
  export PATH=$gm/MacOS:$PATH

- OpenBlas is available via Homebrew.

- The accelerate framework provides optimized BLAS in macOS. To use this with
  gfortran modify LIBBLAS in your Mdefs.mk as follows:

    LIBBLAS = -framework accelerate

  To not load the CPUs to the max for Intel processors you might consider
  setting the number of threads with

    export VECLIB_MAXIMUM_THREADS=n

  where n is the max number of threads. On Apple Silicon (arm64) it is
  recommended to not set VECLIB_MAXIMUM_THREADS to make optimal use of the AMX
  modules on the processor (see file README.macos_arm64).

- Starting with macOS Ventura 13.3 the Accelerate framework supports 64bit
  integers. Just compile tfem with the option -fdefault-integer-8 in
  gfortran and you are done. Although the documentation of Apple mentions
  the setting of the environment variables ACCELERATE_NEW_LAPACK=1 and
  ACCELERATE_LAPACK_ILP64=1, this only affects the vecLib headers.
  It doesn't do anything in tfem and the linking in the framework seems
  to be fully automatic. Tested on Apple Silicon with macOS Sonoma 14.2.1.

- To get proper 'git log' output with colors, set the environment variable:

  export LESS="MR"   # set prompt style (M) and use color in less

- To get the proper vi editor for committing, set the environment variable:

   export GIT_EDITOR='vim -c "set wrapmargin=8"'

- For nagfor I add the following lines to the Mdefs.mk script:

  FCW = -Wc,-Wno-tautological-compare -Wc,-Wno-logical-op-parentheses \
        -Wc,-Wno-incompatible-pointer-types -Wc,-Wno-parentheses

  FFLAGS = $(FCHECK) $(FOPT) $(FDEBUG) $(FMISC) $(FCW)

  to remove many annoying messages from the clang compiler.

- For gfortran I add the following line to the Mdefs.mk script:

  RANLIB = ranlib -no_warning_for_no_symbols

  to get rid of annoying messages about no symbols.

- The metis library is available in Homebrew. However it is int32 only. If you
  need the int64 library, you should compile the library yourself.
